// A context that knows what the hub has of it.
//
// Reading the hub, signing and publishing are all the ledger package. This is
// the readme that explains them to an agent, and the one thing the context
// adds: which package it is.

// Everything to do with the hub's data is the published package.
let hub = @ledger:2:baguqeeragbnpxxmpvxfcteqaxyl24jy6ahbj4woat55begigh5tduvmgadsa

let name = "publishing"

let releases = hub.releases
let signatories = hub.signatories
let publishers = hub.publishers
let owner = hub.owner
let entry = hub.entry
let next = hub.next
let publish = hub.publish

// This context, as the hub has it. `version` is what a new release follows.
let self = (_) -> { hub.latest({package: name}) }

let skills = [
  {
    name: "self",
    description: "self({}) -> Result({package, version, module, published}, String). This context as the hub has it, version 0 and published False means it has never been released."
  },
  {
    name: "releases",
    description: "releases({}) -> Result(List({package, version, module, signatory}), String). Every release, with the signatory that published it."
  },
  {
    name: "signatories",
    description: "signatories({}) -> Result(List({signatory, key, event}), String). Every signatory the hub knows and its key events."
  },
  {
    name: "next",
    description: "next({chain}) -> Result({sequence, previous}, String). What a new entry in a chain must carry. The chain is per publisher, not per package."
  },
  {
    name: "publish",
    description: "publish({key, key_id, signatory, chain, package, module}) -> Result(String, String). Signs a release and submits it. `key` is from CreateKey, `key_id` its base32 id."
  },
  {
    name: "owner",
    description: "owner({package}) -> Result(Option({package, signatory}), String). The signatory the hub says owns a name, None if no owner has been granted."
  },
  {
    name: "entry",
    description: "entry({sequence, previous, key, package, version, module, signatory}) -> String. The exact bytes a signatory signs to publish a release. `previous` is Some(cid) or None({})."
  },
  {
    name: "publishers",
    description: "publishers({package}) -> Result(List(String), String). The signatories that have released under a package name."
  }
]

let readme = "# Publishing on the EYG hub

You are working in a context that knows about itself. It is the `publishing`
package, and it can tell you what the hub has of it and who is allowed to change
that.

The module you are given has these fields.

- `readme` this text.
- `skills` a list of `{name, description}`.
- `self` `({}) -> Result({package, version, module, published}, String)`
- `releases` `({}) -> Result(List({package, version, module, signatory}), String)`
- `signatories` `({}) -> Result(List({signatory, key, event}), String)`
- `publishers` `({package}) -> Result(List(String), String)`
- `owner` `({package}) -> Result(Option({package, signatory}), String)`
- `next` `({chain}) -> Result({sequence, previous}, String)`
- `publish` `({key, key_id, signatory, chain, package, module}) -> Result(String, String)`

There are no other fields, do not guess any.

## What can be inspected

The hub keeps two public ledgers and both can be read without any credentials.

- `/packages/pull` is every release, and every entry names the signatory that
  made it. `releases` and `publishers` read this one.
- `/signatories/pull` is every signatory and the keys added to or removed from
  it. `signatories` reads this one.

So \"who has published under this name\" is answerable, and so is \"who is allowed
to\", they are different questions and have different answers. `publishers` is
history, `owner` is permission. A name with no owner granted answers `None({})`,
which means nobody may publish it yet, not that anybody may.

```eyg
({self, publishers}) -> {
  match self({}) {
    Ok(me) -> {
      match publishers({package: me.package}) {
        Ok(who) -> { {me, who} }
        Error(reason) -> { perform Abort(reason) }
      }
    }
    Error(reason) -> { perform Abort(reason) }
  }
}
```

## Publishing a new version

Most of the way there. A key can be made here with `CreateKey`, `entry` builds
the exact bytes a release is signed over, and `Sign` signs them.

```eyg
({entry}) -> {
  match perform CreateKey(Eddsa({})) {
    Ok(key) -> {
      let bytes = entry({
        sequence: 1,
        previous: None({}),
        key: \\\"the base32 key id\\\",
        package: \\\"example\\\",
        version: 1,
        module: \\\"bafy...\\\",
        signatory: \\\"baguqeera...\\\"
      })
      perform Sign({key, data: !string_to_binary(bytes)})
    }
    Error(reason) -> { perform Abort(reason) }
  }
}
```

`publish` does all of it: `next` reads the chain for the sequence and previous,
`entry` builds the bytes, `Sign` signs them and the signed entry is posted.

```eyg
({publish}) -> {
  match perform CreateKey(Eddsa({})) {
    Ok(key) -> {
      publish({
        key,
        key_id: \\\"the base32 id the signatory registered\\\",
        signatory: \\\"baguqeera...\\\",
        chain: \\\"baguqeera...\\\",
        package: \\\"example\\\",
        module: \\\"bafy...\\\"
      })
    }
    Error(reason) -> { perform Abort(reason) }
  }
}
```

The hub only takes it if `owner` names the signatory publishing it, so check
that first and tell the user to ask for the name rather than watching a 403.
A key made here is new, it is not the one a signatory registered, so publishing
with it fails until the key has been added to that signatory.

```sh
# once, to create a signatory and register its key
eyg signatory initial personal

# every release
eyg publish publishing path/to/index.eyg
```

The version is decided by the hub, it is the next one after the last. Read
`self` first to see what that will be.

Before a signatory can publish under a name, an administrator grants it:

```sh
# on the hub
gleam run -m hub/dev/grant_owner <package> <signatory>
```

Tell the user this plainly if they ask to publish. Do not write a script that
tries, a signature over the wrong bytes is still not a release, and pretending
otherwise wastes their time.
"

{
  readme,
  skills,
  self,
  releases,
  signatories,
  publishers,
  owner,
  entry,
  next,
  publish
}
